  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nMidday water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis \nreveal a fairly amplified pattern by late-summer standards is in \nplace atop the CONUS, with a ~597dm ridge centered over the Desert \nSouthwest and a trough extending from the upper Midwest/Great Lakes \nsouthward through Dixie Alley. At the surface, weak high pressure is \nspread out from the northern plains through the central plains, \nsupporting light east/southeast winds across our area. Despite \nstrong DNVA ahead of the upper ridge, models actually show \ntemperatures will cool by 1-2 degrees at 850-mb thanks to \nnortheasterly flow on the backside of the eastern trough. As a \nresult, the relatively cool airmass in place across southwest KS\nwill persist for another day with afternoon highs in the upper \n80s to low 90s. Tonight, HREF members are nearly split 50/50 \nregarding thunderstorm activity as the NSSL WRF and WRF-ARW \nsuggest an MCS will dive southward through our area while the \nHRRR and 3kmNAM have little to no convection. If the former \nhigh-res guidance verifies, sufficient deep-layer shear and \nMUCAPE will exist for an isolated severe wind gust risk, which \nis consistent with SPC's Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) they have \nlaid out centered around US-283.\n\nDaytime Friday, short range ensembles agree the upper level ridge \nwill inch northeastward but slightly dampen, yielding a stronger \nwesterly component to the flow across the central Rockies. In \nresponse, lee cyclogenesis will begin in east-central CO causing \nstrong southerly winds to materialize across the central plains. \nThis along with 2-3 degrees of warming at 850-mb should translate to \na noticeable warmer afternoon with highs reaching the mid to upper \n90s. Convective activity should be muted under the influence of the \nupstream ridge, however a stray sub-severe thunderstorm or two \nmeandering out of eastern CO cannot be ruled out.\n\nSaturday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nindicate the upper level ridge will continue to shuffle eastward \nbefore stalling out over the southern plains early next business \nweek. This synoptic evolution will yield above normal temperatures \nas highs hover in the upper 90s to low 100s as well as limited \nprecipitation chances. NBM does paint some low-end chance pops (25-\n54%) Sunday and Monday evenings favoring our northwest/north zones, \nhowever disagreement between the ECMWF EPS and GEFS implies these \nare overdone.",
